WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:08.020
&gt;&gt; Welcome Yoshua, to
Microsoft and in Redmond.

00:00:08.021 --> 00:00:11.700
You've been here as part
of our Microsoft AI's

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:14.400
distinguished lecture series and

00:00:14.401 --> 00:00:16.200
gave a fabulous talk yesterday.

00:00:16.201 --> 00:00:16.681
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:00:16.681 --> 00:00:18.470
&gt;&gt; To a standing
room only crowd,

00:00:18.471 --> 00:00:20.160
here and spent time with

00:00:20.161 --> 00:00:22.570
our researchers and
engineers and I want to thank

00:00:22.571 --> 00:00:25.070
you for taking
this opportunity to share

00:00:25.071 --> 00:00:27.890
some of your insights
with a broader community.

00:00:27.891 --> 00:00:30.855
So we're here in part because of

00:00:30.856 --> 00:00:32.510
the role that you've played as

00:00:32.511 --> 00:00:34.680
an amazing leading figure in

00:00:34.681 --> 00:00:38.260
a real transformation that
deep learning has enabled in

00:00:38.261 --> 00:00:40.440
the last 5-10 years
and you think

00:00:40.441 --> 00:00:44.600
about the capabilities that
computers now have from,

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:48.840
speech recognition, speech
generation, image analysis,

00:00:48.841 --> 00:00:53.540
even real-time translation
from one language to another.

00:00:53.541 --> 00:00:55.350
There's a whole
generation of people

00:00:55.351 --> 00:00:57.890
growing up who think that
you can pick up a phone,

00:00:57.891 --> 00:00:59.845
say something and something

00:00:59.846 --> 00:01:01.985
magical will happens
most of the time.

00:01:01.986 --> 00:01:04.325
10 years ago that
was inconceivable

00:01:04.326 --> 00:01:06.255
and you must be proud,

00:01:06.256 --> 00:01:09.015
excited, exhilarated by this.

00:01:09.016 --> 00:01:10.980
But you've also, this is not

00:01:10.981 --> 00:01:12.585
something that you've
taken up recently,

00:01:12.586 --> 00:01:14.915
you've been in the field
for a number of years.

00:01:14.916 --> 00:01:16.270
I did the wrong math yesterday,

00:01:16.271 --> 00:01:17.670
and said 25 years but you

00:01:17.671 --> 00:01:19.615
corrected me and
said it's 30 years.

00:01:19.616 --> 00:01:22.750
Can you say a little bit
about what's

00:01:22.751 --> 00:01:26.610
motivated you over the years
to continue to think about,

00:01:26.611 --> 00:01:29.490
a human intelligence,
machine intelligence and

00:01:29.491 --> 00:01:32.365
how we can leverage
insights from both of those?

00:01:32.366 --> 00:01:38.490
&gt;&gt; Right, so, when I read
these first neural net papers

00:01:38.491 --> 00:01:44.820
around 1985, 1986, it
was passion, I mean,

00:01:44.821 --> 00:01:46.210
like I didn't know what to

00:01:46.211 --> 00:01:48.025
do for my grad studies.
Suddenly I knew.

00:01:48.026 --> 00:01:50.610
&gt;&gt; So these are the Rumelhart,

00:01:50.611 --> 00:01:52.340
McClelland, and early papers

00:01:52.341 --> 00:01:53.765
and Geoff Hinton and
somethings like that.

00:01:53.766 --> 00:01:56.600
&gt;&gt; So Geoff Hinton was
a huge inspiration for me,

00:01:56.601 --> 00:02:00.550
and, yeah, the whole
connectionist groom.

00:02:00.551 --> 00:02:03.315
The PDP book was my Bible.

00:02:03.316 --> 00:02:04.340
&gt;&gt; The two volumes?

00:02:04.341 --> 00:02:06.635
&gt;&gt; The two volumes. Yes, yes.

00:02:06.636 --> 00:02:13.160
One the reasons I
was attracted beyond

00:02:13.161 --> 00:02:21.650
the AI and my adolescent
interest for science fiction,

00:02:21.651 --> 00:02:28.450
was the idea that by studying
this field not only we'll

00:02:28.451 --> 00:02:30.920
get a better understanding of

00:02:30.921 --> 00:02:33.110
intelligence for
building machines

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:34.640
that could be useful for humans.

00:02:34.641 --> 00:02:36.800
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; But also will

00:02:36.801 --> 00:02:39.075
help us to understand
our own intelligence.

00:02:39.076 --> 00:02:40.930
As as intelligence
is something so

00:02:40.931 --> 00:02:42.710
central to what humans means.

00:02:42.711 --> 00:02:45.540
&gt;&gt; You focus mostly on
the former of those?

00:02:45.541 --> 00:02:45.701
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.890
&gt;&gt; You still have
a deep passion for the latter?

00:02:47.891 --> 00:02:51.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah. I mean obviously
I had to make a choice.

00:02:51.041 --> 00:02:53.025
I was more of a nerd.

00:02:53.026 --> 00:02:56.065
Then somebody,

00:02:56.066 --> 00:03:00.215
attracted to psychology
and things like this.

00:03:00.216 --> 00:03:03.340
But over the years,

00:03:03.341 --> 00:03:04.692
probably again

00:03:04.693 --> 00:03:06.975
following the inspiration
from Geoff Hinton,

00:03:06.976 --> 00:03:10.510
I've always kept close proximity

00:03:10.511 --> 00:03:13.975
to neuroscientists,
cognitive scientists.

00:03:13.976 --> 00:03:16.450
As you may know,

00:03:16.451 --> 00:03:21.230
we have set up this program,
Seefar program,

00:03:21.231 --> 00:03:24.245
which brings together
AI machine learning

00:03:24.246 --> 00:03:25.505
researchers with

00:03:25.506 --> 00:03:28.850
neuroscientists
and kind sciences.

00:03:28.851 --> 00:03:31.040
&gt;&gt; Can you share a little bit
about your thesis?

00:03:31.041 --> 00:03:32.870
Because in many ways it bears

00:03:32.871 --> 00:03:35.390
at least a surface
level resemblance

00:03:35.391 --> 00:03:37.250
to many of the things
that are going on today.

00:03:37.251 --> 00:03:39.050
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.935
So in my thesis,

00:03:41.936 --> 00:03:45.990
which I started in 1986,

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:49.110
actually, that was
the master's thesis

00:03:49.111 --> 00:03:50.140
and then I did PhD.

00:03:50.141 --> 00:03:51.980
In Canada, we do masters and PhD

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:53.925
so and I finished in 1991.

00:03:53.926 --> 00:03:57.770
I was, so during
this five years I was working on

00:03:57.771 --> 00:04:01.660
speech recognition using
Recurrent Neural Nets

00:04:01.661 --> 00:04:04.135
with a sort of
convolutional architecture,

00:04:04.136 --> 00:04:07.485
was before modern
architectures, obviously.

00:04:07.486 --> 00:04:12.130
Any Probabilistic Models,
Hidden Markov models,

00:04:12.131 --> 00:04:14.355
with the neural and

00:04:14.356 --> 00:04:16.510
the probabilistic pieces
being integrated,

00:04:16.511 --> 00:04:18.370
that was my main contribution,

00:04:18.371 --> 00:04:20.800
how to train
these things properly.

00:04:20.801 --> 00:04:25.860
It gave me a lot of hands-on

00:04:25.861 --> 00:04:27.960
experience with
the difficulties of

00:04:27.961 --> 00:04:31.275
training neural nets,
and in particular,

00:04:31.276 --> 00:04:34.860
I stumbled upon the difficulties

00:04:34.861 --> 00:04:36.250
or training recurrent nets on

00:04:36.251 --> 00:04:37.960
long sequences and
a few years later,

00:04:37.961 --> 00:04:39.425
I published a number
of papers on

00:04:39.426 --> 00:04:41.960
that subject which became
very, very well cited.

00:04:41.961 --> 00:04:43.380
&gt;&gt; Good.

00:04:43.381 --> 00:04:44.940
So in the last 10 years,

00:04:44.941 --> 00:04:47.280
we've really seen
a transformation of some

00:04:47.281 --> 00:04:49.930
of those ideas and
roots take place.

00:04:49.931 --> 00:04:52.700
What do you think
the top two or three things,

00:04:52.701 --> 00:04:56.810
are either technical or
social that have enabled that?

00:04:56.811 --> 00:04:57.620
&gt;&gt; That have?

00:04:57.621 --> 00:05:00.530
&gt;&gt; Enabled the the amazing
revolution that we've seen in

00:05:00.531 --> 00:05:03.465
the last decade or
so in deep learning.

00:05:03.466 --> 00:05:07.990
&gt;&gt; Well, these things
have been said

00:05:07.991 --> 00:05:09.645
before by many people.

00:05:09.646 --> 00:05:13.860
Of course there's
a alignment of planets

00:05:13.861 --> 00:05:19.390
and its its been technical
progress on the algorithms,

00:05:19.391 --> 00:05:21.990
the amount of data,
the computing power.

00:05:21.991 --> 00:05:24.605
If we focus on the algorithms,

00:05:24.606 --> 00:05:26.520
there is, of course,

00:05:26.521 --> 00:05:28.990
what gave them the name
Deep Learning Depth,

00:05:28.991 --> 00:05:31.430
which has been an intense
subject of study from

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:34.660
the beginning and of interests,

00:05:34.661 --> 00:05:36.645
why would it help,

00:05:36.646 --> 00:05:40.490
and this idea that I
continue pushing today,

00:05:40.491 --> 00:05:43.000
that what we should be striving

00:05:43.001 --> 00:05:46.550
for our machines that discover
better representations,

00:05:46.551 --> 00:05:48.250
that are more and more abstract.

00:05:48.251 --> 00:05:52.250
The other idea that we had
at the beginning was that,

00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:54.810
by stacking these layers
on top of each other,

00:05:54.811 --> 00:05:56.540
we build more complex

00:05:56.541 --> 00:05:58.185
obstructions on top
of simpler ones.

00:05:58.186 --> 00:06:00.995
So that's a very central thing,

00:06:00.996 --> 00:06:02.740
and now we understand

00:06:02.741 --> 00:06:05.145
it better mathematically,
theoretically.

00:06:05.146 --> 00:06:10.230
But there were a few
really important,

00:06:10.231 --> 00:06:16.375
little technical things that
completely changed the way

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:18.520
we are approaching
applications in

00:06:18.521 --> 00:06:21.845
our success in many cases.

00:06:21.846 --> 00:06:26.650
Such as, the use of
rectifiers, the ReLu and so on.

00:06:26.651 --> 00:06:30.900
Actually, my student
Ziegler row and I,

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:32.540
were the first to
show that, hey,

00:06:32.541 --> 00:06:34.710
if you use these piece
wise linear networks,

00:06:34.711 --> 00:06:36.990
you can really train
deep nets whereas,

00:06:36.991 --> 00:06:40.050
if you had the hyperbolic
tangent it didn't work.

00:06:40.051 --> 00:06:42.585
So we don't yet,

00:06:42.586 --> 00:06:43.850
and just that we don't
have a good theory

00:06:43.851 --> 00:06:45.085
to understand that yet.

00:06:45.086 --> 00:06:46.600
This was 2010.

00:06:46.601 --> 00:06:48.040
&gt;&gt; So still lots
of work to be done?

00:06:48.041 --> 00:06:54.025
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah. Then I

00:06:54.026 --> 00:06:57.390
would say another
great contribution

00:06:57.391 --> 00:07:00.560
to today's success
for many models,

00:07:00.561 --> 00:07:03.800
not for computer vision but
more on the language side,

00:07:03.801 --> 00:07:07.040
is the use of
attention mechanisms.

00:07:07.041 --> 00:07:09.470
As you said there's been crucial

00:07:09.471 --> 00:07:11.210
for machine translation
for example,

00:07:11.211 --> 00:07:14.655
but it's now used
everywhere we went to handle

00:07:14.656 --> 00:07:16.820
more complicated
data structures then

00:07:16.821 --> 00:07:21.195
simple images or sequences.

00:07:21.196 --> 00:07:27.210
So that's interesting
because it's changing,

00:07:27.211 --> 00:07:31.160
these things are really
changing the face of

00:07:31.161 --> 00:07:33.380
neural nets as I
was using them in

00:07:33.381 --> 00:07:35.640
the late 80s, during my PhD.

00:07:35.641 --> 00:07:39.690
They used to be pattern
recognition machines and

00:07:39.691 --> 00:07:42.805
the first successes with

00:07:42.806 --> 00:07:44.780
speech recognition for example

00:07:44.781 --> 00:07:47.430
and then with object
recognition in images,

00:07:47.431 --> 00:07:48.600
you're really focused on

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.620
this pattern
recognition ability.

00:07:50.621 --> 00:07:53.425
But if you look at what
people are doing now

00:07:53.426 --> 00:07:58.435
and for example applications in

00:07:58.436 --> 00:08:01.890
natural length like machine
reading comprehension

00:08:01.891 --> 00:08:05.510
and even in
complicated models that

00:08:05.511 --> 00:08:07.910
involve reinforcement
learning or

00:08:07.911 --> 00:08:11.090
memory these notions of

00:08:11.091 --> 00:08:12.860
attention become
really, really useful.

00:08:12.861 --> 00:08:14.475
&gt;&gt; Okay. You shared,

00:08:14.476 --> 00:08:16.110
yesterday with us some of

00:08:16.111 --> 00:08:20.010
your insights about moving

00:08:20.011 --> 00:08:21.810
from the remarkable
progress we've made

00:08:21.811 --> 00:08:23.740
in pattern recognition too

00:08:23.741 --> 00:08:28.335
much higher level cognitive
capabilities in large part by

00:08:28.336 --> 00:08:31.080
abstraction and talked
about a couple of

00:08:31.081 --> 00:08:33.950
what are essentially priors
that one can use to

00:08:33.951 --> 00:08:36.130
constrain what it is that

00:08:36.131 --> 00:08:39.660
an agent acting in the world
would need to learn.

00:08:39.661 --> 00:08:40.980
Can you share some of

00:08:40.981 --> 00:08:42.580
those broad ideas
with the audience?

00:08:42.581 --> 00:08:44.190
&gt;&gt; Sure, absolutely.
So first of all,

00:08:44.191 --> 00:08:46.550
I think the idea
that machine learning

00:08:46.551 --> 00:08:49.220
is built by combining

00:08:49.221 --> 00:08:52.760
the right priors and of course
you know bringing the data

00:08:52.761 --> 00:08:54.730
on top of that is

00:08:54.731 --> 00:08:56.560
something that is not
sufficiently appreciated.

00:08:56.561 --> 00:08:57.945
Some people think that,

00:08:57.946 --> 00:09:00.050
we got this magical
universal recipe

00:09:00.051 --> 00:09:02.175
that's going to solve
all of our problems.

00:09:02.176 --> 00:09:04.425
No, unfortunately.

00:09:04.426 --> 00:09:06.435
There is this the dream of

00:09:06.436 --> 00:09:08.320
Tabula Rasa machine learning but

00:09:08.321 --> 00:09:11.475
it just doesn't
make much sense and

00:09:11.476 --> 00:09:16.385
instead what we're doing in
practice is coming up with

00:09:16.386 --> 00:09:18.440
sometimes very
domain specific and

00:09:18.441 --> 00:09:22.685
hopefully more
general constraints

00:09:22.686 --> 00:09:27.130
or a priority or assumption
that help structure

00:09:27.131 --> 00:09:29.580
the models so that they will
generalize very very well.

00:09:29.581 --> 00:09:32.505
In fact, depth itself is
an example of things like that.

00:09:32.506 --> 00:09:35.010
What I talked about
yesterday are priors

00:09:35.011 --> 00:09:37.290
that we can put in
the architecture,

00:09:37.291 --> 00:09:39.970
the training framework
that hopefully

00:09:39.971 --> 00:09:43.460
help the learners mostly
unsupervised but it

00:09:43.461 --> 00:09:44.720
could be also supervised or

00:09:44.721 --> 00:09:47.360
reinforcement learning
to discover

00:09:47.361 --> 00:09:50.570
better representations
and better means that in

00:09:50.571 --> 00:09:52.990
the represented space you

00:09:52.991 --> 00:09:56.580
could hopefully
predict more easily,

00:09:56.581 --> 00:09:59.500
those predictions could
involve very few variables

00:09:59.501 --> 00:10:01.970
so this is the
consciousness prior or

00:10:01.971 --> 00:10:04.610
these variables would correspond

00:10:04.611 --> 00:10:06.470
closely to aspects of

00:10:06.471 --> 00:10:08.695
the world that can be
controlled by the agent.

00:10:08.696 --> 00:10:11.300
So these are the kinds of ideas

00:10:11.301 --> 00:10:13.860
that I'm exploring these days.

00:10:13.861 --> 00:10:17.115
&gt;&gt; Again, to pop
up a level a bit,

00:10:17.116 --> 00:10:20.730
the goal of a lot of
this work is really

00:10:20.731 --> 00:10:24.710
to enable machines to come from

00:10:24.711 --> 00:10:27.430
some of the very simple
and perceptual tasks

00:10:27.431 --> 00:10:29.910
that they do very well at now to

00:10:29.911 --> 00:10:32.670
achieving the level of

00:10:32.671 --> 00:10:33.870
higher level abstraction in

00:10:33.871 --> 00:10:36.305
common sense that even toddlers.

00:10:36.306 --> 00:10:40.325
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we are far from
building machines that have

00:10:40.326 --> 00:10:44.805
the learning capability
of toddlers.

00:10:44.806 --> 00:10:49.100
We can do a lot of
engineering to solve

00:10:49.101 --> 00:10:51.810
specific problems and
bring in tons of data

00:10:51.811 --> 00:10:53.210
and do a lot better
than toddlers or

00:10:53.211 --> 00:10:55.230
sometimes even
better than adults,

00:10:55.231 --> 00:10:58.020
but toddlers are still hugely

00:10:58.021 --> 00:11:01.630
better at quickly
learning new tasks,

00:11:01.631 --> 00:11:03.110
quickly learning language which

00:11:03.111 --> 00:11:04.330
is one of the things
I'm interested

00:11:04.331 --> 00:11:08.295
in and we don't know
how to manage that.

00:11:08.296 --> 00:11:13.400
So, one thing that
I think we need

00:11:13.401 --> 00:11:18.235
to pay more attention
to is some of

00:11:18.236 --> 00:11:20.970
the older ideas from AI and

00:11:20.971 --> 00:11:25.540
cognitive science which
have been sort of forgotten

00:11:25.541 --> 00:11:27.500
with all this
modern deep learning

00:11:27.501 --> 00:11:30.610
and we need to find

00:11:30.611 --> 00:11:33.660
a way to augment
the deep learning concepts

00:11:33.661 --> 00:11:35.435
that have been very successful

00:11:35.436 --> 00:11:39.480
with some of the objectives
that the eye has set

00:11:39.481 --> 00:11:42.060
itself to solve without

00:11:42.061 --> 00:11:45.550
losing all of the advantages
of better generalization and

00:11:45.551 --> 00:11:48.320
so on capturing what looks like

00:11:48.321 --> 00:11:52.525
intuition which current
deep learning is giving us.

00:11:52.526 --> 00:11:56.080
&gt;&gt; So do you think that
involves a combination of

00:11:56.081 --> 00:11:58.710
neural and symbolic
architectures

00:11:58.711 --> 00:12:00.980
or taking ideas
from each of those?

00:12:00.981 --> 00:12:04.805
What do you think of
the architecture of [inaudible].

00:12:04.806 --> 00:12:08.715
&gt;&gt; There was
a popular set of papers,

00:12:08.716 --> 00:12:12.940
I mean interests
in the 90s trying

00:12:12.941 --> 00:12:15.050
to marry the neural with the

00:12:15.051 --> 00:12:16.210
symbolic and there was like

00:12:16.211 --> 00:12:18.100
workshops and
conferences and so on.

00:12:18.101 --> 00:12:21.400
It didn't really
work. I think maybe

00:12:21.401 --> 00:12:25.485
the mistake was to try to
build some sort of hybrid.

00:12:25.486 --> 00:12:27.450
Whereas, I'm thinking more

00:12:27.451 --> 00:12:34.720
about hybrid at
a more abstract level

00:12:34.721 --> 00:12:36.725
where we take
the concepts that have

00:12:36.726 --> 00:12:39.240
the principles have
worked well in one case

00:12:39.241 --> 00:12:41.365
and in the other case
and how do we build

00:12:41.366 --> 00:12:43.800
architectures that
combine these advantages,

00:12:43.801 --> 00:12:48.700
that for example introduce
in things like neural nets,

00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:51.395
the notion of indirect reference

00:12:51.396 --> 00:12:54.140
which is central to
symbolic processing.

00:12:54.141 --> 00:12:56.465
It's quite feasible

00:12:56.466 --> 00:12:58.050
to design architectures
that have this.

00:12:58.051 --> 00:13:00.220
Is just that people haven't
explored that very much.

00:13:00.221 --> 00:13:02.500
&gt;&gt; So when you say
indirect reference,

00:13:02.501 --> 00:13:04.110
you mean things like no sense of

00:13:04.111 --> 00:13:06.755
objects and name
and events, right?

00:13:06.756 --> 00:13:08.480
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, names right?

00:13:08.481 --> 00:13:11.800
So, a name is something
you can reuse in

00:13:11.801 --> 00:13:16.080
different contexts and
that you can assign values,

00:13:16.081 --> 00:13:22.030
you can do computation
on the name itself.

00:13:22.031 --> 00:13:24.080
&gt;&gt; Right, okay.

00:13:24.081 --> 00:13:29.060
&gt;&gt; So once you bring

00:13:29.061 --> 00:13:30.395
the notion of name in

00:13:30.396 --> 00:13:32.520
the neural net world it becomes

00:13:32.521 --> 00:13:35.255
just something we're familiar
with like embeddings,

00:13:35.256 --> 00:13:37.410
because we want
to be able to name

00:13:37.411 --> 00:13:39.580
a combinatorially
large set of things.

00:13:39.581 --> 00:13:42.085
So now we see this kind
of marriage coming.

00:13:42.086 --> 00:13:46.185
&gt;&gt; Good. You're making
initial strides in

00:13:46.186 --> 00:13:50.335
this area and it seems like
a really difficult challenge,

00:13:50.336 --> 00:13:51.560
partly because we
don't know what

00:13:51.561 --> 00:13:53.540
the objective
functions are partly

00:13:53.541 --> 00:13:55.090
because or they're distributed

00:13:55.091 --> 00:13:56.950
over time in many scenarios.

00:13:56.951 --> 00:13:59.145
Can you just speak a little,

00:13:59.146 --> 00:14:01.020
give folks a glimmer

00:14:01.021 --> 00:14:03.330
of what the future
might hold in that area?

00:14:03.331 --> 00:14:07.100
&gt;&gt; Sure. So what we're trying to

00:14:07.101 --> 00:14:12.930
do is also move machine learning

00:14:12.931 --> 00:14:18.370
away from the raw perceptual
space where all of

00:14:18.371 --> 00:14:21.480
our current training
objectives are located

00:14:21.481 --> 00:14:25.475
like likelihood or
even the gan objectives.

00:14:25.476 --> 00:14:27.635
They're all like in pixel space.

00:14:27.636 --> 00:14:31.770
Instead, if you consider
what humans value,

00:14:31.771 --> 00:14:34.590
how humans plan, how

00:14:34.591 --> 00:14:36.380
we check that our predictions

00:14:36.381 --> 00:14:38.200
are good and what kind of
predictions we're making,

00:14:38.201 --> 00:14:40.385
it's all happening in
this abstract space.

00:14:40.386 --> 00:14:42.920
We don't make t to t plus

00:14:42.921 --> 00:14:45.030
one predictions and
unfold all of this.

00:14:45.031 --> 00:14:50.125
Like directly predict
things in some vague future

00:14:50.126 --> 00:14:52.030
and we do it in

00:14:52.031 --> 00:14:54.700
this very abstract space

00:14:54.701 --> 00:14:57.225
which doesn't need to
describe every pixel.

00:14:57.226 --> 00:15:00.335
So this endeavor with,

00:15:00.336 --> 00:15:03.180
for example the
consciousness prior is

00:15:03.181 --> 00:15:05.940
trying to design or presentation

00:15:05.941 --> 00:15:08.140
learning mechanisms that where

00:15:08.141 --> 00:15:10.440
the objective function
is defined in

00:15:10.441 --> 00:15:15.060
that space and that could be

00:15:15.061 --> 00:15:17.520
used for example in
reinforcement learning

00:15:17.521 --> 00:15:21.270
where planning or unfolding into

00:15:21.271 --> 00:15:23.785
the future doesn't need to be

00:15:23.786 --> 00:15:27.950
happening in like playing
a movie in your head.

00:15:27.951 --> 00:15:29.595
That's not what's happening in

00:15:29.596 --> 00:15:32.680
people's mind but
rather something

00:15:32.681 --> 00:15:37.780
abstract that can
refer to only some,

00:15:37.781 --> 00:15:39.620
only needs to refer
to some aspects of

00:15:39.621 --> 00:15:41.490
the world and not every
little detail of it.

00:15:41.491 --> 00:15:44.765
&gt;&gt; Okay. So, is work for
example in Program Induction,

00:15:44.766 --> 00:15:46.540
central to these ideas or was

00:15:46.541 --> 00:15:48.250
that a different
way of approaching

00:15:48.251 --> 00:15:51.870
some of these you know
the ability to act in the world,

00:15:51.871 --> 00:15:54.060
understand the consequences
of your actions,

00:15:54.061 --> 00:15:55.480
predict what might happen

00:15:55.481 --> 00:16:01.220
&gt;&gt; So, I think in
Program Induction there is

00:16:01.221 --> 00:16:05.290
an approach in which
we're working purely

00:16:05.291 --> 00:16:07.240
syntactically which like we're

00:16:07.241 --> 00:16:09.940
doing with our
current deep learning,

00:16:09.941 --> 00:16:13.710
where we're modeling
sequences of characters that

00:16:13.711 --> 00:16:17.530
somehow do correspond to

00:16:17.531 --> 00:16:20.375
programs and I don't believe
this is going to work.

00:16:20.376 --> 00:16:24.290
I think for program induction
and as well for general AI,

00:16:24.291 --> 00:16:26.870
what we need is grounded
language learning.

00:16:26.871 --> 00:16:29.600
In other words that
the learner figures

00:16:29.601 --> 00:16:31.940
out at the same time as it's

00:16:31.941 --> 00:16:34.305
learning the language
like that the symbols

00:16:34.306 --> 00:16:36.375
that are being manipulated

00:16:36.376 --> 00:16:38.660
is learning what they refer to.

00:16:38.661 --> 00:16:40.760
In the case of
Program Induction that would

00:16:40.761 --> 00:16:43.765
be what the computation
corresponds to

00:16:43.766 --> 00:16:46.370
and then learns
to somehow reason

00:16:46.371 --> 00:16:49.455
and act with that knowledge.

00:16:49.456 --> 00:16:52.050
In general language learning,

00:16:52.051 --> 00:16:54.520
I think this is
something that's been

00:16:54.521 --> 00:16:56.200
discussed for many years and I

00:16:56.201 --> 00:16:57.980
see more and more papers
in that direction.

00:16:57.981 --> 00:17:00.510
I'm very encouraged
by that directions

00:17:00.511 --> 00:17:03.310
from the community and I
want to push that as well.

00:17:03.311 --> 00:17:06.970
&gt;&gt; I have a few general
questions that I want to pop to,

00:17:06.971 --> 00:17:09.420
I mean thank you
for sharing some of

00:17:09.421 --> 00:17:12.830
the insights about how
you got into this area,

00:17:12.831 --> 00:17:18.340
your decade long pursuit
and understanding how

00:17:18.341 --> 00:17:20.595
people learn using
inspirations from that

00:17:20.596 --> 00:17:24.320
to support the development
of more intelligent machines.

00:17:24.321 --> 00:17:27.060
But I want to focus on
a couple of other things.

00:17:27.061 --> 00:17:30.160
So one would be you
right now in Montreal

00:17:30.161 --> 00:17:34.480
as a Professor at
the University of Montreal,

00:17:34.481 --> 00:17:38.025
as the head of the MILA
or broader organization,

00:17:38.026 --> 00:17:44.010
as an important member
in this community,

00:17:44.011 --> 00:17:48.475
I have seen lots of students
and lots of collaborators.

00:17:48.476 --> 00:17:51.520
Is there any advice
that you can offer to

00:17:51.521 --> 00:17:53.120
new people who are interested in

00:17:53.121 --> 00:17:55.260
the field both at
a technical level,

00:17:55.261 --> 00:17:57.740
so maybe people who'll
develop the next algorithms,

00:17:57.741 --> 00:18:00.420
but also people who are thinking
about how to use some of

00:18:00.421 --> 00:18:03.667
the insights shape insights
for their problems.

00:18:03.668 --> 00:18:06.560
&gt;&gt; So, first of all,

00:18:06.561 --> 00:18:10.235
there's a lot of information
about AI machine learning,

00:18:10.236 --> 00:18:12.160
deep learning, personal learning

00:18:12.161 --> 00:18:14.475
available out there for free.

00:18:14.476 --> 00:18:17.475
I often get, undergrad coming to

00:18:17.476 --> 00:18:21.780
me from poor countries even,

00:18:21.781 --> 00:18:25.025
who got a lot of their
information from the Internet.

00:18:25.026 --> 00:18:28.665
There's so many things,
tutorials, videos,

00:18:28.666 --> 00:18:30.245
papers of course, everything is

00:18:30.246 --> 00:18:33.170
an archive in
our field, it's amazing.

00:18:33.171 --> 00:18:37.520
And libraries, open source code,

00:18:37.521 --> 00:18:41.585
so there's a lot of opportunity
to learn by yourself,

00:18:41.586 --> 00:18:45.165
and if you can
afford a GPU card,

00:18:45.166 --> 00:18:46.530
even like a cheap one,

00:18:46.531 --> 00:18:48.065
you can actually do quite a lot.

00:18:48.066 --> 00:18:49.335
In terms of learning,
you won't be able to

00:18:49.336 --> 00:18:51.730
compete in some of
these harder benchmarks,

00:18:51.731 --> 00:18:52.870
you can learn a lot.

00:18:52.871 --> 00:18:58.035
You can understand how
new net training is happening.

00:18:58.036 --> 00:19:01.235
So there's a lot that an
individual who's motivated,

00:19:01.236 --> 00:19:04.790
and has basics in
computer science and math,

00:19:04.791 --> 00:19:07.125
can actually learn
by themselves.

00:19:07.126 --> 00:19:12.925
That being said, I see
an amazing progression of,

00:19:12.926 --> 00:19:14.215
say, some of
those students who are

00:19:14.216 --> 00:19:16.250
coming to labs like mine,

00:19:16.251 --> 00:19:18.645
and after six months, a year,

00:19:18.646 --> 00:19:22.800
or two years of
working in a group,

00:19:22.801 --> 00:19:28.405
and there's this synergy
that happens in the learning,

00:19:28.406 --> 00:19:31.430
the presence of people
who know more than you,

00:19:31.431 --> 00:19:36.255
environments in which
you can speak freely,

00:19:36.256 --> 00:19:38.490
you can express your ideas
and not feel judged.

00:19:38.491 --> 00:19:38.836
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:19:38.836 --> 00:19:43.905
&gt;&gt; I think, transform
these people

00:19:43.906 --> 00:19:47.510
into researchers very quickly,

00:19:47.511 --> 00:19:52.705
and so, if you have a chance
to go in an existing group,

00:19:52.706 --> 00:19:56.455
I think it could be
a big step in your learning.

00:19:56.456 --> 00:20:01.205
So, this is why students
do grad studies.

00:20:01.206 --> 00:20:03.015
I know these days,

00:20:03.016 --> 00:20:06.215
it might be tempting for
many people to directly go

00:20:06.216 --> 00:20:11.255
from undergrad or masters
level training, to industry.

00:20:11.256 --> 00:20:14.255
But you don't necessarily

00:20:14.256 --> 00:20:16.565
get that if you're
going to especially,

00:20:16.566 --> 00:20:19.390
become an engineer who
applies these things.

00:20:19.391 --> 00:20:21.185
So, depending on
where you want to go,

00:20:21.186 --> 00:20:24.730
I think it's something
to think about.

00:20:24.731 --> 00:20:26.385
&gt;&gt; That sounds like
foundational skills,

00:20:26.386 --> 00:20:29.665
hands-on practice for
people who are more,

00:20:29.666 --> 00:20:32.845
maybe researching client,
even being part of a larger,

00:20:32.846 --> 00:20:36.100
intellectual and open
and sharing community.

00:20:36.101 --> 00:20:36.905
&gt;&gt; And reading.

00:20:36.906 --> 00:20:37.535
&gt;&gt; Reading. Okay.

00:20:37.536 --> 00:20:39.670
&gt;&gt; Reading. I mean
this is not new.

00:20:39.671 --> 00:20:41.685
Any scientists will tell you.

00:20:41.686 --> 00:20:43.075
Right? You can't even

00:20:43.076 --> 00:20:45.285
stay on top of
your science if you don't

00:20:45.286 --> 00:20:50.775
read at least 20 percent
of your time.

00:20:50.776 --> 00:20:52.295
In our field,

00:20:52.296 --> 00:20:54.055
things are moving so fast.

00:20:54.056 --> 00:20:55.355
I can't keep up with everything

00:20:55.356 --> 00:20:58.800
that's being written obviously.

00:20:58.801 --> 00:21:01.670
So, this is a social network,

00:21:01.671 --> 00:21:04.705
I get to know what
to read because I

00:21:04.706 --> 00:21:07.185
talk to other people
and I tell them,

00:21:07.186 --> 00:21:09.135
"I read this and
you should read it",

00:21:09.136 --> 00:21:11.325
and some of that happens online.

00:21:11.326 --> 00:21:16.580
So, reading and getting
inspiration like reading groups,

00:21:16.581 --> 00:21:19.335
in order to have

00:21:19.336 --> 00:21:22.305
guidance about what to
read, is really important.

00:21:22.306 --> 00:21:25.825
There are nice things
like OpenReview.net

00:21:25.826 --> 00:21:26.286
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:21:26.286 --> 00:21:28.455
&gt;&gt; Where, people in our field

00:21:28.456 --> 00:21:31.335
in the SCLR community
submit their papers,

00:21:31.336 --> 00:21:32.645
and then anyone can comment,

00:21:32.646 --> 00:21:35.165
and you can read
the reviews online.

00:21:35.166 --> 00:21:38.055
This is amazing. This is great.

00:21:38.056 --> 00:21:40.025
It allows for
more participation,

00:21:40.026 --> 00:21:42.025
and allows for
people to see how the

00:21:42.026 --> 00:21:44.725
process of submitting
papers and reviewing,

00:21:44.726 --> 00:21:47.395
and analyzing what's going on,

00:21:47.396 --> 00:21:48.845
and also just knowing, okay,

00:21:48.846 --> 00:21:50.540
I probably don't
want to read this,

00:21:50.541 --> 00:21:53.065
or I didn't expect
this I should read it.

00:21:53.066 --> 00:21:54.235
You don't need to read
the whole paper to

00:21:54.236 --> 00:21:55.585
get a sense of what's in there.

00:21:55.586 --> 00:21:58.385
&gt;&gt; You also learn a lot by
people's comments on papers.

00:21:58.386 --> 00:21:59.115
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:21:59.116 --> 00:22:00.885
&gt;&gt; It's really interesting

00:22:00.886 --> 00:22:02.635
to see some of that in
the OpenReview comments.

00:22:02.636 --> 00:22:05.285
&gt;&gt; And you can have
anonymous comments so that.

00:22:05.286 --> 00:22:06.155
&gt;&gt; For better or worse.

00:22:06.156 --> 00:22:07.145
&gt;&gt; For better or worse.

00:22:07.146 --> 00:22:11.705
There are positives
and negatives.

00:22:11.706 --> 00:22:13.455
&gt;&gt; Reflecting about
the changes that have

00:22:13.456 --> 00:22:15.875
happened in the last
decade to support

00:22:15.876 --> 00:22:20.805
some real advancements in
machine learning and AI,

00:22:20.806 --> 00:22:22.575
one of the things you
didn't mention that

00:22:22.576 --> 00:22:24.955
I think is really

00:22:24.956 --> 00:22:26.775
important and that
you've spent a lot of

00:22:26.776 --> 00:22:29.755
your energy supporting
is a really open,

00:22:29.756 --> 00:22:32.485
agile and collaborative
environment

00:22:32.486 --> 00:22:35.835
whether it's through
tools with Theano.

00:22:35.836 --> 00:22:37.845
Through the book, that you

00:22:37.846 --> 00:22:41.340
and Ian Goodfellow
and Aaron Courville

00:22:41.341 --> 00:22:44.075
published that is one of

00:22:44.076 --> 00:22:47.440
the best sellers on Amazon
but also more importantly,

00:22:47.441 --> 00:22:49.365
the chapters are
available for anybody.

00:22:49.366 --> 00:22:49.746
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for free.

00:22:49.746 --> 00:22:52.855
&gt;&gt; To read without carrying
necessarily the book

00:22:52.856 --> 00:22:55.665
around to new conferences

00:22:55.666 --> 00:22:57.485
to focus on
representation learning,

00:22:57.486 --> 00:23:00.425
the OpenReview process even

00:23:00.426 --> 00:23:03.435
publishing early in
archive all of those have

00:23:03.436 --> 00:23:06.270
created I think a new energy in

00:23:06.271 --> 00:23:09.840
AI and machine learning
that wasn't there before.

00:23:09.841 --> 00:23:12.905
Can you comment on how?

00:23:12.906 --> 00:23:15.075
&gt;&gt; I'm not the only one there,

00:23:15.076 --> 00:23:16.445
it's a community thing.

00:23:16.446 --> 00:23:16.646
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:16.646 --> 00:23:18.085
&gt;&gt; Actually you
forgot one thing,

00:23:18.086 --> 00:23:19.645
in the late 90s,

00:23:19.646 --> 00:23:21.740
I perceived it in the revolt of

00:23:21.741 --> 00:23:24.235
the machine learning
journal board

00:23:24.236 --> 00:23:25.950
that led to
the creation of GMLR,

00:23:25.951 --> 00:23:28.015
which is now
the flagship journal.

00:23:28.016 --> 00:23:30.545
&gt;&gt; So, you've always
had this revel streak.

00:23:30.546 --> 00:23:31.775
&gt;&gt; Oh yeah.

00:23:31.776 --> 00:23:34.165
&gt;&gt; Maybe, open
collaborative community.

00:23:34.166 --> 00:23:35.135
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's right.

00:23:35.136 --> 00:23:39.075
&gt;&gt; That has led to
a lot of the both to

00:23:39.076 --> 00:23:40.745
the insights but also perhaps to

00:23:40.746 --> 00:23:43.480
a frenetic and
unmaintainable pace so, yeah.

00:23:43.481 --> 00:23:46.865
&gt;&gt; There are good sides
and bad sides here.

00:23:46.866 --> 00:23:47.326
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:23:47.326 --> 00:23:48.665
&gt;&gt; I think overall,

00:23:48.666 --> 00:23:50.820
it's helping
the progress of science.

00:23:50.821 --> 00:23:51.400
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:23:51.401 --> 00:23:54.185
&gt;&gt; It's helping

00:23:54.186 --> 00:23:56.340
making science more
accessible as well,

00:23:56.341 --> 00:23:58.630
which is something I care about.

00:23:58.631 --> 00:24:02.550
So some of what you're
talking about is

00:24:02.551 --> 00:24:06.900
associated with a sense of
responsibility that I think,

00:24:06.901 --> 00:24:08.985
Scientists should have and

00:24:08.986 --> 00:24:11.595
especially as they
mature in their career.

00:24:11.596 --> 00:24:15.705
We can give back and

00:24:15.706 --> 00:24:18.285
the things that matter are not

00:24:18.286 --> 00:24:20.605
just how many papers
we published.

00:24:20.606 --> 00:24:23.475
&gt;&gt; That's hard sometimes for

00:24:23.476 --> 00:24:25.615
a young graduate student looking

00:24:25.616 --> 00:24:28.465
for a position to appreciate.

00:24:28.466 --> 00:24:29.085
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:24:29.086 --> 00:24:35.400
&gt;&gt; So, let me ask a final
question that builds on that so,

00:24:35.401 --> 00:24:38.025
it's clear now that AI is out of

00:24:38.026 --> 00:24:42.325
its academic box and really
having a profound impact on

00:24:42.326 --> 00:24:46.290
many aspects of society
you hear about it

00:24:46.291 --> 00:24:49.505
in all sorts of things

00:24:49.506 --> 00:24:52.695
ranging from fake news
to issues of fairness,

00:24:52.696 --> 00:24:55.260
accountability, and
transparency and ethics.

00:24:55.261 --> 00:24:57.245
Do you have any insights

00:24:57.246 --> 00:24:59.580
about how we as
a research community,

00:24:59.581 --> 00:25:01.665
an intellectual community can

00:25:01.666 --> 00:25:04.405
help shape those outcomes
for the better?

00:25:04.406 --> 00:25:06.555
&gt;&gt; I think there
are many ways in

00:25:06.556 --> 00:25:10.265
which researchers in
this field can have

00:25:10.266 --> 00:25:12.105
a positive contribution to

00:25:12.106 --> 00:25:17.900
the social impact and
the ethical aspects and how,

00:25:17.901 --> 00:25:19.495
what we call AI for good,

00:25:19.496 --> 00:25:23.545
how AI could be used for
the good of humanity in general.

00:25:23.546 --> 00:25:29.055
I see it's amazing.

00:25:29.056 --> 00:25:33.125
A very rapid increase
in the interest in

00:25:33.126 --> 00:25:37.720
these questions over the last
12 months even two years.

00:25:37.721 --> 00:25:40.425
One of the aspects
that I'm thinking

00:25:40.426 --> 00:25:43.185
more about these days is

00:25:43.186 --> 00:25:46.915
how we should try to
shift the kinds of

00:25:46.916 --> 00:25:50.985
machine learning problems
on which we work towards,

00:25:50.986 --> 00:25:53.340
like object
recognition on ImageNet

00:25:53.341 --> 00:25:57.085
towards problems that could
have a big positive impact in

00:25:57.086 --> 00:25:58.455
the world and
especially problems

00:25:58.456 --> 00:26:00.205
that companies traditionally

00:26:00.206 --> 00:26:01.725
are not really interested
in because there may not

00:26:01.726 --> 00:26:03.825
be any profit in the short term.

00:26:03.826 --> 00:26:06.735
Like humanitarian applications.

00:26:06.736 --> 00:26:12.415
So, that's something I've
been thinking about and how

00:26:12.416 --> 00:26:15.545
we could help
the poorest countries

00:26:15.546 --> 00:26:18.880
with technology with machine
learning with our skills.

00:26:18.881 --> 00:26:20.955
How we could help the people in

00:26:20.956 --> 00:26:22.785
those countries
learn those skills

00:26:22.786 --> 00:26:24.705
so that they can
help themselves.

00:26:24.706 --> 00:26:27.355
I think there's a lot that
could be done and because

00:26:27.356 --> 00:26:30.155
these problems are not
necessarily for profit,

00:26:30.156 --> 00:26:32.795
it would be easier
for companies,

00:26:32.796 --> 00:26:36.605
the big IT companies to
actually collaborate on

00:26:36.606 --> 00:26:39.465
these questions than
the traditional applications

00:26:39.466 --> 00:26:41.555
where they're really
competing against each other.

00:26:41.556 --> 00:26:47.005
&gt;&gt; So, let's close on
that call to action,

00:26:47.006 --> 00:26:49.425
for the machine learning and AI

00:26:49.426 --> 00:26:52.835
community to not just continue
our technical excellence,

00:26:52.836 --> 00:26:54.915
but understand how we can really

00:26:54.916 --> 00:26:57.340
shape people and society

00:26:57.341 --> 00:26:58.705
which is certainly one
of our passions here.

00:26:58.706 --> 00:26:59.555
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much Susan.

00:26:59.556 --> 00:27:00.875
&gt;&gt; Thank you so much for

00:27:00.876 --> 00:27:03.135
your visit and for
this wonderful talk.

00:27:03.136 --> 00:27:04.125
&gt;&gt; My pleasure.

00:27:04.126 --> 00:27:07.875
&gt;&gt; Thanks Yoshua.

